<div style="clear:both;"></div>

## Interpreting a Prediction Line

A separate example from the lesson shows how to actually read a prediction off a line: 
- locate Miles = 4 on the x-axis
- trace up to the line
- read across to find the cost, $9.

<div style="text-align: center; margin: 20px auto;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/0998a77b6f6cd420088a447b61f410a4-image-1794328966531.png" alt="A graph titled Interpreting a Prediction Line, with Miles on the x-axis and Cost in dollars on the y-axis, showing dashed lines reading a prediction of $9 at 4 miles" style="width: 55%; border-radius: 10px;">
</div>

## The Equation Behind the Line

Every prediction line follows the same formula, and that formula translates directly into Python.

<div style="border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; margin: 12px 0 16px 0; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8;">
  <div style="background-color: #1F1976; padding: 16px 20px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8; line-height: 1.8;">
y = mx + b
  </div>
</div>

<div style="border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; margin: 12px 0 16px 0; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8;">
  <div style="background-color: #1F1976; padding: 16px 20px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8; line-height: 1.8;">
m = 1.5<br>
b = 3<br>
<br>
def predict(x):<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;return m * x + b
  </div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>&#128161; Teacher tip:</strong> this is the exact shape every regression function in this unit takes, multiply the input by the slope, then add the y-intercept. You'll write a version of this yourself in the Python Lab.</div>
</div>



<div style="background-color: #D5EFFF; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin-top: 20px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #06338D; line-height: 1.6;">
    <strong>&#128188; Career context:</strong> a Machine Learning Engineer writes code that uses data to build models capable of making useful predictions, exactly what students do in this lesson's Python implementation.
  </div>
</div>
MARKDOWN
